# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: clang-format, a search for switched-off checks, clang-tidy, a
build with warnings as errors, and the tests with AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and
a hardened standard library. It also reads what the compiler really got: a flag outside its list,
a file the gates skip, a library from the machine, a test property, a skipped test or a test
that did not finish with a passing GoogleTest report fails it.
The git hook and CI run it too.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, `.clang-tidy`, `.clang-format`, `.clangd`,
  `CMakePresets.json`, `cmake/`, `mise.toml`, `mise.lock`, the standard and the include order in
  `CMakeLists.txt`, and existing tests.

The commands below need `mise trust` once in each new git worktree; `scripts/check` does not.

```sh
mise exec --locked -- cmake --build --preset dev        # build (after one scripts/check)
mise exec --locked -- ctest --preset dev -R 'Greet\.'   # tests whose name matches
build/dev/{{namespace}}_test --gtest_filter='Greet.*'     # the same, from the test binary
scripts/format                                           # format
```

Sandboxed agents: mise writes to `~/.local/share/mise`, `~/.local/state/mise` and its cache
(`~/Library/Caches/mise` on macOS, `~/.cache/mise` on Linux). The first run downloads the tools
and the test libraries and needs the network. So does the first check in each new git worktree:
it downloads and builds GoogleTest and rapidcheck again into that worktree's `build/` (about 20 s).

## C++

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing C++.

The checks enforce C++23 without compiler extensions, include what you use
(`misc-include-cleaner`), no C-style casts, no implicit narrowing, and internal linkage for
helpers. They also reject in code (comments and strings are not searched): pragmas other than
`#pragma once`, token pasting (`##`), `asm`, names outside ASCII, `#define` or `#undef` of a
reserved name, `#if` on any macro except `__APPLE__`, `__linux__`, `__cplusplus`, `__cpp_*` and
`__has_include(<header>)`, `#include` by macro, an alias of the `std` namespace or
`using namespace std;`, and `std::abort`, `std::terminate` and `_Exit` (call a member of that
name as `this->abort()`).
CMake files may use only the commands on the list in `scripts/check` (the usual project, target
and test commands; not `execute_process`, `add_custom_command`, `configure_file` or writing
`file()` modes), `include` only named modules and project `.cmake` files, and `add_subdirectory`
only project folders. A trace of what CMake ran is checked the same way, so a file or command
built at run time counts too. `project()` says `LANGUAGES CXX`, and CMake code leaves the
GoogleTest and rapidcheck targets alone. The compiler may read nothing from `build/` but
GoogleTest and rapidcheck, and only files under `tests/` may use them.
Every tool comes from `mise.toml`: run it through `mise exec -- <tool>`. Never call Apple's
clang.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library under `src/`. `app/main.cpp` only calls it.
- `tests/` holds GoogleTest tests and rapidcheck properties (`RC_GTEST_PROP`). Only files under
  `tests/` may include GoogleTest or rapidcheck. Each test
  executable links `GTest::gtest_main` and is registered with `gtest_discover_tests`, with no
  extra arguments. The check runs every test that each one lists.
- C++ files end in `.cpp` (source) or `.hpp` (header). The check rejects other extensions, and
  any `.cpp` or `.hpp` that nothing compiles.
- Add every new `.cpp` to `CMakeLists.txt` by name, without globbing. Define new targets below
  `include(cmake/strict.cmake)`, so they get the strict flags.
- A new dependency goes in `cmake/dependencies.cmake` with an exact URL and SHA-256, after the
  owner approves it.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
